# IRQ-time accounting and network tail latency: experiment (2026-11-24)

**Status:** research-only benchmark, not an upstream patch or a regression
fix. The benchmark is intentionally excluded from the selftests Makefile.
It measures a single-host TCP loopback workload; it **cannot** establish
the published paper's hardware-based latency claims.

## Why investigate this?

Tianyu Zuo et al., *Understanding Host Network Stack Latency*
(SIGCOMM 2026; DOI: 10.1145/3789240.3829115) identified a mismatch:
scheduler accounting may occur during a softirq while IRQ time is only
refreshed at softirq exit. Thus task clock and virtual runtime can be
charged to the wrong task, inflating network P99.9 latency under load.
The authors report up to 2.7x lower tail latency in their testbed
after more accurate IRQ time accounting.

Paper and artifact:
- https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~ragarwal/pubs/understanding-latency.pdf
- https://github.com/Terabit-Ethernet/understand_latency
- https://github.com/Terabit-Ethernet/understand_latency/blob/main/linux-6.12-latency.patch

On Linux master at 8d7fe9885c62fc9a95bf55d9db244d9672a8aadc
(2026-11-24), kernel/sched/core.c's update_rq_clock_task() still
documents delayed IRQ accounting. The published experimental patch is
against Linux 6.12 and includes unrelated scheduler/transport experiments
and a hard-coded IP address. It must not be blindly cherry-picked.

## How to build/run this benchmark

    cc -std=gnu11 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -pthread \
        tools/testing/selftests/sched/irqtime_loopback_bench.c \
        -o /tmp/irqtime_loopback_bench
    /tmp/irqtime_loopback_bench -c 1 -n 10000 -p 64
    /tmp/irqtime_loopback_bench -c 8 -n 10000 -p 64 -b 8

To create real CPU contention, run using taskset(1) on a *permitted*
small CPU set, e.g., taskset -c 2 (only if CPU 2 is allowed by cpuset).
Repeat at least ten times on each kernel, alternate test order, and
record CPU model, governors, kernel config, boot parameters, IRQ affinity,
NUMA placement and confidence intervals. Do not claim paper
reproduction from loopback data. A real-NIC two-host test, high softirq
load, and a trusted model of attributed IRQ time are still required.

The benchmark reports TCP round-trip latencies (average, exact
nearest-rank P50/P95/P99/P99.9) and request counts from a collection of
one-client/one-server TCP connections. It is workload instrumentation,
not a generic scheduler score. Scheduling noise means results can vary
sharply even between identical consecutive runs.

### Local smoke-test evidence (not a kernel comparison)

On the analysis container (Linux 6.18.57, not the 2026 upstream tree):

- gcc -std=gnu11 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -pthread: pass
- 2 clients x 1,000 requests; 64 bytes: 2,000 successful exchanges
- 4 clients x 1,000 requests with 2 busy workers: 4,000 successful exchanges
- gcc -fsanitize=undefined: 3 clients x 1,000 requests with one busy
  worker; 3,000 successful exchanges, no sanitizer diagnostic

These runs establish only that the benchmark compiles and exchanges
correct TCP data. They do not validate an improvement to Linux.

## Merge requirements

1. Reproduce the scheduler's wrong-task IRQ-time attribution on current
   kernels using traces (not just a noisy latency histogram).
2. Establish which existing upstream patches overlap, especially IRQ
   accounting changes and core-scheduler updates from 2024-2026.
3. Derive a correctness-preserving fix for nested hard/softirqs, ksoftirqd,
   preempt-RT, per-CPU clocks, remote rq updates and virtualization.
4. Build/run KUnit and relevant x86/arm64 configs, verify no lost/double
   accounting, compare P99.9 and throughput with at least 10 repetitions.
5. Send an RFC to the scheduler maintainers with explicit attribution to
   Zuo et al. only after independent verification. No guarantee of merge.

Do not send the published paper's experimental patch as our own work.

## Other large issues discovered, not claimed as new

- ext4 silent data loss on ENOSPC during unwritten-extent end-I/O split.
  Reported 2026-11-16 by Jose Fernandez, with significant active
  maintainer involvement. Do not replicate on a real filesystem.
  https://lists.openwall.net/linux-kernel/2026/11/16/223
- fs/pipe ~18.5% regression after unifying per-pipe preallocation pool:
  already being investigated by Breno Leitao et al.; there is already
  an in-tree pipe benchmark and a proposed user-access grouping patch.
  https://lists.openwall.net/linux-kernel/2026/11/05/135
- mm/filemap persistent mmap readahead disabled after sparse faults:
  two overlapping fixes proposed on 2026-11-09; avoid duplicating them.
  https://lists.openwall.net/linux-kernel/2026/11/09/1152

This work does not rely on RH or Dirichlet GRH. We did not identify an
RH-derived algorithm that would materially improve Linux's hot paths.
